{
  "rationale": "The merged front is 17/17 iterative, and iterative also holds the lowest feasible area (269 vs 458 unrolled_k, 1068 pipelined_m, 1876 pipelined). The throughput constraint (>=1 MSPS) is trivially satisfied by iterative even at the slowest schedule (N=30 -> ~33 cycles/result, still tens of MSPS at Artix-7 Fmax), so the only binding constraint is accuracy (max_abs_err <= 2^-10, i.e. accuracy_bits >= 10) and the only objective that matters for selection is luts_plus_ffs. unrolled_k computes identical bits but chains k micro-rotations per cycle, so for the same (W,N) it costs ~k times the datapath and can never beat iterative on area; its 0/17 front membership confirms it is dominated. I therefore put ~95% of the budget on iterative and only a token share on unrolled_k to confirm no surprise. Ranges span the full useful W/N window so NSGA-II can trace the whole trade-off curve: the low-area corner (small W/N, guards minimal, round) that the selection rule will pick, up to the HV reference area (1500) where larger W/N push accuracy toward ~24 bits. angle_guard and frac_guard are opened over their full ranges because they trade area for accuracy independently of W/N and are the cheapest knobs near the accuracy boundary.",
  "families": [
    {
      "why": "Sole owner of the merged front and the area winner. Broad W/N range maps the full curve from the min-area feasible corner (~269 luts+ffs, ~10 bits) up to the HV reference area (1500, high accuracy). Full guard ranges let the search find the cheapest way to clear the 2^-10 error bound at each area point; both rounding modes included since round is essentially free accuracy near the boundary.",
