{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "next_plan": null
  ,"rationale": "Round 1 shows a feasible front that is entirely iterative (unrolled_k: 0/5 feasible, best accuracy 9.56 bits < the required 10 bits, so nothing to add there yet) and covers luts_plus_ffs 298..584 against an HV reference of 1500, and accuracy_bits 11.8..23.3 against an HV reference of 10. Two things are clearly unmapped: (1) the low-accuracy band just above the constraint (accuracy_bits ~10..11.8), which is exactly where the spec's selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs, max_abs_err <= 2^-10) will land, so the 298 luts+ffs point is very likely not the true area floor; (2) the whole bandwidth of the trade-off curve below 298 and across 11.8 bits, even though throughput is a non-issue (best seen 15.6 MSPS vs >= 1 required) and 24% of round-1 samples already violated the error bound, leaving a lot of room near the accuracy constraint. With 100 of 200 evaluations remaining and the front spanning only one family and one corner of both objectives, mapping the full-range front seeded with these 21 designs is the highest-value use of the budget for hypervolume and for the selection rule alike; narrowing now would risk locking onto a non-minimal area point. Stop is premature (first round, all figures estimates) and infeasible is contradicted by 76 feasible evaluations."
}
